# Skill: rust_clippy_by_application

## Why

`RUST_CLIPPY_SCAN_CONFIG=true` makes every Rust package run its own `cargo clippy`.
A **library** scanned standalone has no `Cargo.lock`, so cargo re-resolves to the latest
crates → a newer rustc is demanded → the scan fails under a pinned rustc. The normal build
passes, because it builds through the application's lock. Pinning MSRV per package is
whack-a-mole.

**Core fix:** stop scanning libraries standalone. Scan only **applications (BIN crates)**,
and make each application lint its whole local dependency closure via
`RUSTC_WRAPPER=clippy-driver cargo check`. The application's `Cargo.lock` then governs all
its libraries (no re-resolution), and the libraries are linted with the application's real
`--features`.

**Why `clippy-driver`, not `cargo clippy`:** `cargo clippy` only lints workspace members
(`RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER`); `cargo clippy -p <path-dep>` prints `Checking` but does NOT run
clippy on it. MXNOS libraries are path deps in scattered `*-custom` directories, so the only
way to lint them is `RUSTC_WRAPPER=clippy-driver` — it applies clippy-driver to EVERY compiled
crate. crates.io deps are silenced by cargo's automatic `--cap-lints allow`, so only local
libraries surface.

## Step 1 — Classify application vs library

A package is an **application (BIN)** if its directory has `src/main.rs`, `src/bin/`, or
`[[bin]]` in `Cargo.toml`; otherwise it is a library.

```bash
cd buildroot/dl
find . -maxdepth 4 -name Cargo.toml -not -path '*/target/*' | while read m; do d=$(dirname "$m");
  { [ -f "$d/src/main.rs" ] || [ -d "$d/src/bin" ] || grep -q '^\[\[bin\]\]' "$m"; } && echo "BIN $d"; done
```

Depth 4 is required — it is what catches `3rdparty_net_snmp/snmp_script_rust/<bin>` and
`plugin_*/app`. Name is not a classifier: `plugin_moxa_relay` holds both a BIN (`app/`) and a
library (`framework/`).

## Step 2 — dl Makefile

**Application** `rust_clippy_scan` — convert
`cargo clippy … --target-dir=$RUST_TARGET_DIR [--features …]` to:

```
RUSTC_WRAPPER=clippy-driver cargo check --release --target=${TARGET} --target-dir=${RUST_TARGET_DIR} [--features ${CARGO_FEATURES}]
```

Both the plain line and the `--message-format=json > rust_clippy_scan_result/<name>.json`
line. For plugins with an app+framework split, keep the `app` scan and **remove the
`framework` scan lines**.

**Library** — remove the whole `rust_clippy_scan:` block and unhook it from `all:`. Each dl
repo is its own git repo; commit `clippy scan binaries only`.

## Step 3 — `${moxa}` package `.mk`

For each **library** `moxa/package/net/<pkg>/<pkg>.mk`:

- Remove the `@if [ "${RUST_CLIPPY_SCAN_CONFIG}" = "true" ]; … cp -rf $(@D)/rust_clippy_scan_result … fi` block.
- Remove `RUST_TARGET_DIR=$(BR2_MOXA_RUST_TARGET_DIR)` **only when the scan was its sole
  consumer** — check the dl Makefile first. Some libraries use the shared target dir for the
  normal build too; removing it there silently loses the build-time/space optimisation.
- **Keep both** for application `.mk` (the app scan still uses them).

Buildroot derives the package name from the **directory** and includes `.mk` by `wildcard`,
so a misnamed `.mk` still works but breaks tooling — flag it, don't rely on it.

## Step 4 — post-build.sh gate

`moxa/board/net/common/post-build.sh`:

1. Hard gate: every `.mk` that still exports `rust_clippy_scan_result` (= the applications,
   derived dynamically) must produce its json. Guard with
   `[ -d "${BUILD_DIR}/${pkg}-custom" ] || continue` so packages this product does not build
   are skipped, and skip dummy packages (`rust_moxa_create_dummy_package.sh` placeholders own
   a `-custom` dir but compile nothing).
2. Informational only: a Rust library not referenced by any application prints a `Note:` —
   no exempt list, no failure. A library depends on an application; not being scanned is not
   an error. Match coverage via both `<pkg>-custom` (path deps) and `/<pkg>#` (symlinked
   workspace members such as `rust_moxa_build`'s).
3. Initialise `exit_code=0` before the `check_rust_pkg_br_dependency.py` call.

## Step 5 — verify

Append a warn-level lint to a library's `src/lib.rs` (or `framework/src/lib.rs`), rebuild that
library, run an application scan, expect the warning attributed to that library, then restore.
`cargo check`/`build` do NOT report it — only clippy does, which proves clippy actually ran.

```rust
pub fn clippy_scan_canary() { return; }   // triggers warn-level clippy::needless_return
```

If the scan json already carry real diagnostics attributed to library crates, that is stronger
evidence than a canary and no canary is needed.

Coverage is **feature/config-dependent**: a package that is a dummy in one product will not be
scanned there. Verify on the same defconfig the build uses. Gotcha: a shared `RUST_TARGET_DIR`
caches a no-lint check — `touch` the library source to force a re-lint.

## Step 6 — sync application `Cargo.lock` (build → dl)

Verify each **application's** committed lock is current. If a path dependency's `Cargo.toml`
gained deps, cargo re-resolves at build time and rewrites the lock in
`output/build/<pkg>-custom/`, which never flows back to `dl/`. A stale committed lock means
every clean build re-resolves to *latest-compatible* — non-reproducible, and the MSRV failure
returns.

```bash
scripts/check_rust_app_lock.sh <buildroot>   # exit 1 if any app's dl ≠ output/build lock
# for each drifted app: cp output/build/<pkg>-custom/.../Cargo.lock  dl/<pkg>/.../Cargo.lock ; commit "Synchonize Cargo.lock"
```

Tell-tale of a stale lock in the build log: `Updating crates.io index` /
`Locking N packages to latest compatible versions` / `Adding …`. **Applications only** —
libraries carry no committed lock. These syncs become a **separate** MR (Step 7), kept out of
the clippy MR.

The script reports "0 warnings" when a package has **no** `dl/` lock at all, because it skips
the comparison. That is a false green: treat "dl has no Cargo.lock" as a failure, not a skip.

### Keeping locks current when someone changes a library

The lock lives in the **application's** repo; the `Cargo.toml` gets changed in the
**library's** repo. Whoever changes the library cannot see who depends on it, and CI only
notices at the next integration build. Two layers, and only the second is a guarantee:

- `which_locks_need_update.sh <lib>` (or `--changed [<base-ref>]`) reverse-maps
  library → applications straight out of the committed locks — a `[[package]]` block with no
  `source =` field is a local path dependency. No build needed, so the developer knows the
  blast radius before pushing. Ergonomics, not enforcement.
- `check_rust_app_lock.sh` in CI on the integration build is the enforcement: cargo rewrites
  `output/build/<app>-custom/Cargo.lock` on the next resolve, the check sees dl ≠ output/build
  and fails naming the application. In a multi-repo layout this is the only place that can
  catch it — a library's own pipeline cannot build its downstream applications.

Do not decide the lock source by "dl has at least one lock". Half-way through Step 6 only a few
applications have committed theirs, and scanning just those reports that a library has no
dependants at all. Resolve per application: prefer the dl lock, fall back to `output/build`,
and say which is which.

The real cost is process, not tooling: one library dependency change becomes 1 library MR plus
N application-lock MRs that must merge together, and the locks can only be regenerated by an
actual resolve — change the library, build, copy the changed locks back to `dl/`, commit them
in the application repos.

### Can `--locked` be turned on?

Committing the lock is what buys reproducibility; `--locked` only enforces that nothing
rewrites it silently. So commit first, then enforce — and enforce it on both `cargo build`
and the `clippy-driver cargo check` scan line, or the scan can still re-resolve.

But one committed lock cannot serve every product. The dummy-package mechanism replaces a
plugin this product does not enable with a stub carrying no dependencies, so that plugin's
whole transitive subtree disappears from the graph. The manifests cargo reads therefore differ
per defconfig, and `--locked` that passes for one product fails for another. This is
structural, not drift.

`compare_app_locks.sh <buildroot1> <buildroot2> …` decides this per application. Point it at
workspaces already built with different defconfigs (it labels them from `BR2_DEFCONFIG`), and
it separates the two kinds of difference, which mean opposite things:

- **package set differs** → the graph is product-dependent → that application cannot take a
  single `--locked` lock; give it a per-product lock or leave it on drift detection.
- **same package, different version** → resolution is unstable across builds → direct evidence
  that the lock is missing, and the strongest reason to commit one.

Compare version *sets* per package name, never a join on the name alone: one lock legitimately
carries several majors of the same crate (`regex` 0.2 alongside 1.x), and a name-only join
cross-products them into phantom drift.

A release branch is where `--locked` belongs most — pinning rustc while leaving dependencies
floating is worse than pinning neither, because that is exactly the combination that breaks a
frozen branch when a crate publishes a higher MSRV. But a release branch here still carries
many defconfigs, so run `compare_app_locks.sh` across all of them first and enable `--locked`
only on the product-independent applications. For a branch that must rebuild years later,
`--locked` is not enough on its own — it pins versions, not availability; `cargo vendor` plus
`--frozen` is the end state.

## Step 7 — MR rollout

`scripts/` — all DRY-RUN by default, `--go` to act, `SLEEP=3` to avoid the GitLab
issue-creation rate limit, `TOKEN_FILE=` for a PAT with `api` scope:

- `create_clippy_lib_mrs.sh` — library: open issue → branch `<iid>-<suffix>` from the source
  branch, amend its single commit to `Issue #<iid>: …`, push, MR.
- `create_clippy_app_mrs.sh` — application: branch from **fetched** `origin/<target>`, then
  `git checkout <srcbranch> -- '(^|/)Makefile$'` (Makefile-only → excludes `Cargo.lock`,
  squashes stale `-p`/duplicate commits), single commit, MR.
- `create_cargolock_mrs.sh` — separate `Cargo.lock`-sync MR.
- `check_rust_app_lock.sh` — flags dl↔output/build lock drift, and a missing `dl/` lock.
- `preflight.sh` — run this **before** `--go`; see below.

Everything a new product branch needs is an environment variable — nothing is edited in
place: `DL`, `SRC_BR`, `BASE`, `TARGET`, `TITLE`, `MR_SUFFIX`, `LIST_FILE`, `TOKEN_FILE`,
`SLEEP`. `MR_SUFFIX` and `TITLE` **must** be changed per product line, for the reasons in the
pre-flight table.

Merge via API: `PUT /projects/:enc/merge_requests/:iid/merge`, retrying while
`detailed_merge_status=checking`. Merge the dl MRs **before** the `${moxa}` MR — the
post-build gate added there expects the converted application scans to exist.

### Pre-flight

```bash
DL=… SRC_BR=… BASE=origin/<target> TARGET=<target> TITLE=… MR_SUFFIX=… \
  LIST_FILE=/abs/path/list.txt ./preflight.sh --mode app|lib
```

Read-only; exits 1 on any FAIL. It automates the table below — each row is a failure hit in
production, and none of them is visible by eye across a couple of hundred repos.

| Check | Why |
|---|---|
| Every repo has a **local** branch ref for the source branch | The scripts resolve `$SRC_BR` locally. Buildroot's fetch leaves repos in detached HEAD, so without `git branch -f <src> origin/<src>` they are all silently skipped. |
| Idempotency glob is scoped to this rollout's branch suffix | A previous product line's `<iid>-clippy-scan-binary-packages` branches still exist and match a loose `*-clippy-scan-binary-packages` glob → everything reports `EXISTS` and is skipped. |
| Issue title is unique to this rollout | The scripts reuse an existing **open** issue with the same title. Old rollout issues stay open when the target was not the default branch, so a new rollout silently attaches to them. |
| For each application, does `origin/<target>` also touch `Makefile` since the source branch diverged? | `git checkout <br> -- Makefile` copies the **whole file**, it does not merge. Any target-side Makefile change is **silently reverted** inside a commit titled "clippy". Cherry-pick would conflict; this will not. Handle such repos by hand: branch from the target and apply only the clippy hunk. |
| Does the target already contain the same change? | A repo may have received the clippy change through another route; its MR would then carry only noise and conflict. Drop it from the list. |
| `LIST_FILE` is an absolute path | The scripts `cd "$DL"` before reading it; a relative path yields a silent no-op run. |
| Every commit is single and Makefile-only | `git show --format= --name-only HEAD` per repo. Compare against the target's *tip*, not the merge-base, or target-side commits appear as spurious reversals. |

## Gotchas

| Gotcha | Detail |
|---|---|
| `cargo clippy -p <lib>` | Does NOT lint path deps (only prints `Checking`). Must use `RUSTC_WRAPPER=clippy-driver`. |
| project path regex | Some repos' `git remote get-url origin` has **no `.git` suffix** → a regex requiring `\.git$` yields a malformed path → GitLab API 404 "Project Not Found" (looks like a permission problem). Use `sed -E 's#^.*gitlab\.com[:/]##; s#\.git$##; s#/#%2F#g'`. |
| repo name ≠ package name | The buildroot package directory need not match the git repo (`3rdparty_rust_dhcpd_parser` → `3rdparty_rust_dhcpd-parser.git`; `lib_moxa_rust_intel_temp_ctrl` → `lib_moxa_rust_intelligent_temperature_control_shm.git`). Never guess a URL — take it from `BR2_PACKAGE_<PKG>_URL` in the package's `Config.in`, or from a workspace that already has it checked out. |
| issue-creation rate limit | Rapid POSTs fail intermittently even with access. Use `SLEEP=3`; a re-run is idempotent and resumes. |
| issue auto-close | `Closes #N` only auto-closes when merging to the **default branch**. Targeting a non-default release branch leaves the issues open forever. |
| token format | The PAT file may be `<label>glpat-<PAT>`; extract with `grep -oE 'glpat-[A-Za-z0-9._-]+'`. |
| `Cargo.toml` stays | Never remove a library's `Cargo.toml` — it defines the crate. Only the scan machinery and MSRV add-ons (`rust-version`, standalone `.cargo/config.toml`) become removable. |
| stale `Cargo.lock` | A gitignored `Cargo.lock` left in `dl/<pkg>/` is invisible to `git status` but is rsynced into the build tree, so cargo honours it and the MSRV fix never runs. Tell-tale: the error appears with NO `Updating crates.io index` / `Locking N packages` before it. |
| inventory without fetching | To audit which repos a product needs, `make <product>_defconfig` resolves the full package set (`select` expanded), and `BR2_PACKAGE_<PKG>_URL` gives each repo — no source download required. Back up `.config` first. |
| Rust-ness detection | Neither the `/rust/` URL group nor the `.mk` referencing `RUSTC_TARGET_NAME` is reliable alone: some `/rust/` repos hold no crate at all, and some real Rust apps live outside it. Confirm with `Cargo.toml`. |

## Durable alternative to per-package pinning

Per-package MSRV pinning is whack-a-mole. The root cure is freezing the dependency set: a
committed `Cargo.lock` per application (plus by-application scanning, so libraries never
resolve standalone) and CI `--locked`; strongest is `cargo vendor`. `resolver="3"` /
`incompatible-rust-versions="fallback"` only reduce the risk, they never remove it.
